Umbra-Billy Meridia
Ingredients: ROCKABILLY & ROCK 'N' ROLL + GARAGE ROCK + 432Hz RELAXING MEDITATIVE STYLES + Shaker
Creative Catalyst: It descends from carnival musicians who perform only during solar eclipses.

Sound Description
Imagine a grease-slicked, tube-amplified 1950s Gretsch guitar playing slapback licks that have been hollowed out and tuned precisely to a 432Hz meditative drone, rattling with the frantic, blown-out speaker distortion of a basement garage rock band. Beneath the roaring, heavily compressed tape-delay vocals and manic walking basslines lies an omnipresent, hypnotic bamboo shaker, played with the terrifying precision of a monk counting down the seconds to total darkness. It is an impossible hybrid of hip-swiveling danger and trancelike celestial surrender, sounding like Gene Vincent fronting a psychedelic caravan that has broken down directly in the path of totality.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 142 BPM, driven by an unhinged, galloping rockabilly train-beat that is constantly fighting against a sluggish, meditative 432Hz cyclical undertow.
- Harmonic Palette: Pentatonic rock 'n' roll blues scales drifting unnervingly into just-intonation modal drone territory, utilizing dissonant minor seconds that somehow resolve into blissful, transcendental breathing intervals.
- Production Signatures: Overdriven tube preamps glowing cherry-red and melting onto magnetic tape; a suffocating, claustrophobic garage room reverb pierced by crystal-clear, binaural high-frequency shaker percussions; vocals processed through rotary speaker Leslie cabinets submerged in buckets of mineral oil.
- Dynamics: Abrupt transitions from whisper-quiet, transcendental 432Hz harmonium hums into explosive, feedback-drenched garage rockabilly avalanches whenever the moon takes a single bite out of the sun.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the dust-choked shadow strips of the American Southwest during the 1970 annular eclipse, this movement was forged by rogue sideshow roustabouts who refused to pack up their tents when the moon swallowed the light. They wear grease-stained velvet duster coats over vintage bowling shirts, heavy welder’s goggles fitted with amber lenses, and pocket watches that only run backward during celestial alignments. Their rituals involve setting up broken calliopes in abandoned desert highway diners, waiting for the exact second of totality to strike up a frantic, howling tune that they believe keeps the sun from disappearing forever.

Typical Instruments
- Lead Guitar: 1957 hollow-body electric with heavy palm-muting and vibrating spring reverb.
- Rhythm Section: Upright slap bass played with catgut strings and a distorted solid-state practice amp.
- Atmospheric Core: 432Hz custom-tuned singing crystal bowls integrated directly into the vacuum tube circuitry.
- Percussion: Hand-carved bamboo seed shakers amplified through ribbon microphones placed inside empty oil drums.

